eight=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0;border-bottom:10px solid #f5f5f5\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>      <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 10px;line-height:1.5\">\r\n<span class=\"b k\" style=\"font-size:27px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;line-height:1.5\">  1 big thing: Video stores&#x27; unexpected comeback </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>   <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td> <img src=\"https://images.axios.com/HwzsVoLQ6KMeWfOTZndxkWir6Qw=/1920x1080/smart/2025/06/02/152646-1748878006200.jpg\" width=\"600\" style=\"width:100%\" id=\"item02\" alt=\"Illustration of a row of theater seats set up with a lamp, throw pillow, and cat, as if it is in a house. \"> </td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.3\">\r\n<span class=\"b\" style=\"font-size:13px;color:#848484\"> <p>Illustration: Maura Losch/Axios</p> </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"15\" style=\"height:15px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr> <tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.75;font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px\"><p><strong>Video stores were written off </strong>as casualties of the steaming era — but they're staging a comeback, <em>Axios Local reporters tell us from cities around America.</em></p><p><strong>Why it matters: </strong>New rental shops are opening around the country as people rediscover the fun of browsing the shelves for movie night. </p><ul><li><strong>Plus,</strong> as streaming prices <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47651077.422300/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tL21lZGlhLXRyZW5kcy1tZW1iZXJzaGlwLzIwMjYvMDYvMTMvc3RyZWFtaW5ncy1hbnRpLWNhYmxlLXByb2JsZW0_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB466632ce\" target=\"_blank\">rise</a>, digital licenses lapse and online archives disappear, many cinephiles are returning to physical media for guaranteed access to both new and beloved films.</li></ul><p><strong>🍿 Blockbuster's days are over. </strong>Just one independently owned store remains, in Bend, Ore. But a new generation of video stores is picking up where it left off.</p><ul><li><strong>In the Twin Cities, </strong>nonprofit Saturn Video will open next month, stocked with hard-to-find DVDs, Blu-Rays and VHS tapes, <em>Axios Twin Cities' Audrey Kennedy </em><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47651077.\" target=\"_blank\"><em>reports</em></a><em>.</em></li><li><strong>In Richmond, Va., </strong><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47651077.422300/aHR0cHM6Ly9ibHVlcm9zZXZpZGVvLmNvbS8_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBd5f2386d\" target=\"_blank\">Blue Rose Video &amp; More</a> opened earlier this month, selling DVDs, Blu-rays and 4Ks, and offering DVD rentals. It's at least the third new video store to open in the Richmond area this year, <em>Axios Richmond's Karri Peifer </em><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47651077.422300/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tL2xvY2FsL3JpY2htb25kLzIwMjYvMDkvMjIvY2hlc3RlcmZpZWxkLWR2ZC1ibHUtcmF5LXJlbnRhbHMtYmx1ZS1yb3NlLXJpY2htb25kP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB86056d2b\" target=\"_blank\"><em>reports</em></a><em>.</em></li><li><strong>In Philadelphia, </strong>Little Movie Store — set to open next month — has a 20,000-title collection for the city's physical media fans to peruse, <em>Philly Voice </em><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47651077.422300/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cucGhpbGx5dm9pY2UuY29tL2xpdHRsZS1tb3ZpZS1zdG9yZS12aWRlby1yZW50YWwtc291dGgtcGhpbGx5LW9wZW5pbmcvP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBeaafab1a\" target=\"_blank\"><em>reports</em></a><em>.</em></li><li><strong>In Sacramento, </strong>Very Good Video is opening this fall to serve as a rental store and a community gathering spot, hosting screenings, trivia and more, <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47651077.422300/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuc2FjYmVlLmNvbS9uZXdzL2J1c2luZXNzL2FydGljbGUzMTY3NjI1NTcuaHRtbD91dG1fc291cmNlPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXImdXRtX21lZGl1bT1lbWFpbCZ1dG1fY2FtcGFpZ249bmV3c2xldHRlcl9heGlvc2ZpbmlzaGxpbmUmc3RyZWFtPXRvcA/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB4fde1f02\" target=\"_blank\"><em>reports</em></a><em> The Sacramento Bee.</em></li></ul><p><strong>👀 The intrigue: </strong>Some of the biggest fans of the video store revival are young people.</p><ul><li><strong>Video stores hit</strong> a sweet spot for Gen Zers, many of whom are just old enough to remember wandering the aisles and picking out a DVD as kids, <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47651077.\" target=\"_blank\"><em>notes the LA Times</em></a><em>.</em></li></ul><p><strong>The bottom line: </strong>The nostalgia economy is booming, and video stores are the latest comeback story.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47651077.422300/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tL25ld3NsZXR0ZXJzL2xvY2FsP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB37a05992\" target=\"_blank\"><em>Find your Axios Local city</em></a><em>.</em></li></ul></td>\r\n</tr> <tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47651077.422300/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_dT1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vbmV3c2xldHRlcnMvYXhpb3MtZmluaXNoLWxpbmUtNmJjOGZlZTEtMzZkNC00OWEzLWJkZmEtNDZlZThhYjlmNTA4Lmh0bWw_Y2h1bmslM0QxJTI2dXRtX3Rlcm0lM0RmYnNvY2lhbHNoYXJlJT